<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!--
        No permissions are declared, on purpose.

        The Storage Access Framework grants access to the documents the user
        picks, through a URI permission on the result Intent. Declaring
        READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE, READ_MEDIA_* or MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE would ask
        the user for broad storage access this plugin does not need and must not
        have. An app that depends on this plugin inherits no permission from it.
    -->

    <application>
        <!--
            The transparent activity that runs the ACTION_OPEN_DOCUMENT round
            trip. DartNative exposes no Activity-result hook, so the plugin owns
            this one; see FilePickerProxyActivity for the full rationale.

            exported=false        only this plugin starts it.
            Translucent theme     the user never sees it, only the system picker.
            configChanges         a rotation while the picker is up must not
                                  recreate the proxy and relaunch the picker.

            NOTE on taskAffinity: this activity deliberately keeps the app's
            DEFAULT task affinity. It must NOT declare android:taskAffinity=""
            (it did, and that was a bug found on device: the NEW_TASK launch
            landed in a task of its own, so finishing the proxy tore down a task
            that was not the user's and returned them to the wrong place).

            Task affinity is NOT what makes a picked document readable
            afterwards, and an earlier version of this comment claimed it was.
            Measured on device (finding F9, physical Galaxy S23 Ultra, Android
            16): the transient URI permission that ACTION_OPEN_DOCUMENT returns
            is owned by the ACTIVITY that received the result, not by its task,
            and AOSP revokes it when that activity leaves the history stack
            (ActivityRecord.removeFromHistory -> removeUriPermissionsLocked).
            This proxy finishes the moment it has the result, so the transient
            grant is gone milliseconds later and no task arrangement saves it.

            Durability therefore comes from somewhere else: reference mode takes
            a READ-ONLY persistable URI grant while the proxy still holds the
            transient one, and SessionGrants owns the lifetime of every grant
            the caller did not ask to persist, releasing it at the next process
            start and on engine detach. See SessionGrants.kt for the full
            argument and the alternatives that were rejected.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name="com.dartnative.file_picker.FilePickerProxyActivity"
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar"
            android:exported="false"
            android:noHistory="false"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|uiMode" />
    </application>
</manifest>
